<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">San Lorenzo</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 BCE – 900 BCE (approx.) · capital San Lorenzo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The earliest great Olmec centre, on the Coatzacoalcos river, where colossal stone heads and the first large-scale planned settlement in Mesoamerica appeared.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Farming villages on a raised plateau above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coatzacoalcos-river">Coatzacoalcos</a> river floodplain in southern Veracruz grew into a major centre from about 1400 BCE. Control of fertile levee land and river trade routes allowed an elite to emerge, and by 1200 BCE San Lorenzo was the largest settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers are known only through sculpture: ten colossal basalt heads, thought to be portraits of chiefs, and large table-thrones. Basalt was hauled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a> some 60 kilometres away, showing great command of labour. The plateau was artificially levelled and fitted with stone drains. Obsidian, magnetite and fine pottery moved through wide exchange networks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 900 BCE San Lorenzo declined, possibly through river shifts or political change, and many monuments were deliberately defaced and buried. Leadership of the Olmec world passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/la-venta">La Venta</a>. Its sculptural style shaped later Mesoamerican art and kingship.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/san-lorenzo.jpg" alt="San Lorenzo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CabezaColosal1_MuseoXalapa_v1.1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 BCE – 900 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Lorenzo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Olmec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably Mixe-Zoquean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>San Lorenzo Tenochtitlan, Olmec San Lorenzo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1150&amp;focus=san-lorenzo" aria-label="San Lorenzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2107 1520 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where San Lorenzo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2427 1776L2450 1762"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2450 1762h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2427 1776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2427 1776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1150&amp;focus=san-lorenzo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
